2025-05-01 02:10:24 - Thread-699 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Below you find  

1) a mapping of every field that appears in the “Traffic Fatalities Info List 2015” to the concrete column(s) that live in the three 2015 FARS tables, together with possible conditions you will often need; and  

2) ten SHORT sample‑queries (all `LIMIT 20`) that go from “look around” to “mini‑analysis”.  
Every query is prefaced with a short comment that explains what it does.

======================================================================
A.  Mapping of the requested critical details
======================================================================

```
consecutive_number           : Key to merge the three files (`state_number`,`consecutive_number`)
county                        : `county`  (all three tables) – location of crash
type_of_intersection         : `type_of_intersection`               (accident_2015)
light_condition              : `light_condition`,`light_condition_name` (accident_2015)
atmospheric_conditions_1     : `atmospheric_conditions_1` / `_name` (accident_2015)
hour_of_crash                : `hour_of_crash`                      (accident, person, vehicle)
functional_system            : `functional_system` / `_name`        (accident_2015)
related_factors              : `related_factors_crash_level_1`      (accident_2015)

delay_to_hospital            : hour difference between `hour_of_ems_arrival_at_hospital`
                               and `hour_of_crash`  (accident_2015)
                               – Use: WHERE hour_of_ems_arrival_at_hospital BETWEEN 0 AND 23

delay_to_scene               : hour difference between `hour_of_arrival_at_scene`
                               and `hour_of_crash`  (accident_2015)
                               – Use: WHERE hour_of_arrival_at_scene BETWEEN 0 AND 23

age                          : `age`                                (person_2015)
person_type                  : `person_type` / `_name`              (person_2015)
seating_position             : `seating_position` / `_name`         (person_2015)
restraint                    : `restraint_system_helmet_use`        (person_2015)
survived                     : derive from `injury_severity`
                               – survived = 1 if injury_severity = 4 (possible injury),
                                 0 otherwise

rollover (vehicle level)     : `rollover`                           (vehicle_2015)  
                               – binary: 0 “No Rollover”, else 1

airbag                       : `air_bag_deployed`                   (person_2015)
                               – binary: 1 when value BETWEEN 1 AND 9

alcohol                      : `police_reported_alcohol_involvement` (person_2015)
                               – binary: 1 when value = '1'

drugs                        : `police_reported_drug_involvement`    (person_2015)
                               – binary: 1 when value = '1'

related_factors_person_level1: `related_factors_person_level1`       (person_2015)

travel_speed                 : `travel_speed`                        (vehicle_2015)

speeding_related             : `speeding_related`                    (vehicle_2015)
                               – binary: 1 when LIKE 'Yes%'

extent_of_damage             : `extent_of_damage`                    (vehicle_2015)
body_type                    : `body_type` / `_name`                 (vehicle_2015)
vehicle_removal              : `vehicle_removal`                     (vehicle_2015)

manner_of_collision          : `manner_of_collision`                 (accident_2015, vehicle_2015, person_2015)
                               – if need “capped at 11”: use `LEAST(manner_of_collision,11)`

roadway_surface_condition    : `roadway_surface_condition`           (vehicle_2015)
                               – if need “capped at 8”: use `LEAST(roadway_surface_condition,8)`

first_harmful_event          : `first_harmful_event`                 (all 3 tables)  
                               – keep value if < 90 else set 0

most_harmful_event           : `most_harmful_event`                  (vehicle_2015)  
                               – keep value if < 90 else set 0
```

======================================================================
B.  Ten illustrative BigQuery SQL snippets
======================================================================

```sql
-- 1) Show 20 different counties that appear in the accident file
SELECT DISTINCT county
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015`
WHERE county <> 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Count crashes by lighting condition (top 20)
SELECT light_condition_name, COUNT(*) AS crashes
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015`
GROUP BY light_condition_name
ORDER BY crashes DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Quick look at weather vs. crashes during night (hour_of_crash 0‑5)
SELECT atmospheric_conditions_1_name AS weather, COUNT(*) AS night_crashes
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015`
WHERE hour_of_crash BETWEEN 0 AND 5
GROUP BY weather
ORDER BY night_crashes DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Compute the two delay metrics for accidents with good timestamps
SELECT
  state_number,
  consecutive_number,
  (hour_of_arrival_at_scene  - hour_of_crash)             AS delay_to_scene,
  (hour_of_ems_arrival_at_hospital - hour_of_crash)       AS delay_to_hospital
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015`
WHERE hour_of_arrival_at_scene        BETWEEN 0 AND 23
  AND hour_of_ems_arrival_at_hospital BETWEEN 0 AND 23
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Vehicles that rolled over and their estimated travel speed
SELECT
  v.state_number,
  v.consecutive_number,
  v.vehicle_number,
  v.travel_speed,
  v.rollover
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2015` AS v
WHERE v.rollover <> '0'            -- 1 = rollover event
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) People who survived (injury_severity = 4) and whether their airbag deployed
SELECT
  p.state_number,
  p.consecutive_number,
  p.person_number,
  p.injury_severity,
  p.air_bag_deployed,
  CASE WHEN p.injury_severity = 4 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS survived,
  CASE WHEN p.air_bag_deployed BETWEEN 1 AND 9 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS airbag_binary
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.person_2015` AS p
WHERE p.injury_severity IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Speeding‑related vehicles that also involved alcohol (driver drinking = '1')
SELECT
  v.state_number,
  v.consecutive_number,
  v.vehicle_number,
  v.speeding_related,
  v.driver_drinking
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2015` AS v
WHERE v.speeding_related LIKE 'Yes%'   -- 2,3,4,5 values all start with 'Yes'
  AND v.driver_drinking = '1'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Join vehicle + accident to see light condition for each speeding crash
SELECT
  a.state_name,
  v.consecutive_number,
  a.light_condition_name,
  v.travel_speed,
  v.speeding_related
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2015`   AS v
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015`  AS a
USING (state_number, consecutive_number)
WHERE v.speeding_related LIKE 'Yes%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Combine person + vehicle to look at restraint use vs. rollover
SELECT
  p.state_number,
  p.consecutive_number,
  p.person_number,
  p.restraint_system_helmet_use,
  CASE WHEN p.restraint_system_helmet_use = 0 THEN 'No Restraint'
       WHEN p.restraint_system_helmet_use = 1 THEN 'Shoulder Belt Only'
       WHEN p.restraint_system_helmet_use = 2 THEN 'Lap Belt Only'
       WHEN p.restraint_system_helmet_use = 3 THEN 'Lap‑Shoulder Belt'
       ELSE 'Other/Unknown' END AS restraint_desc,
  v.rollover
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.person_2015`  AS p
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.vehicle_2015` AS v
ON  p.state_number       = v.state_number
AND p.consecutive_number = v.consecutive_number
AND p.vehicle_number     = v.vehicle_number
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) State‑level summary: fatal crashes, drunk drivers, avg delay to hospital
SELECT
  a.state_name,
  COUNT(DISTINCT a.consecutive_number)                             AS fatal_crashes,
  SUM(a.number_of_drunk_drivers)                                   AS drunk_drivers,
  AVG(CASE WHEN a.hour_of_ems_arrival_at_hospital BETWEEN 0 AND 23
           THEN (a.hour_of_ems_arrival_at_hospital - a.hour_of_crash)
      END) AS avg_delay_to_hospital_hours
FROM `bigquery-public-data.nhtsa_traffic_fatalities.accident_2015` AS a
GROUP BY a.state_name
ORDER BY fatal_crashes DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These examples should give you a quick but complete starting point for exploring the 2015 traffic‑fatality data set and reproducing every item in the “critical details” list.
